  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nUpper low over Nova Scotia will not move much today with a spoke\nof energy rotating north to south across the area this evening.\nThis spoke of energy will increasing clouds this morning across\nmuch of the area as well as chances for showers this afternoon\nand evening. CAMs are in decent agreement that showers will\nmostly be confined to the mountains and interior NH while the\ncoastal plain remains mostly dry. There may be enough\ninstability for an isolated thunderstorm across southwest NH.\nCloud cover and cooler NE flow will keep highs in the 70s.\nShower chances will linger over NH into tonight with trend\ntowards mostly clear skies towards Sunday morning.\n\nHigh pressure over the Great Lakes will gradually build in\nSunday, although with upper low still nearby an isolated shower\ncannot be ruled out. The incoming high pressure will favor more\nsunshine than today while highs will be in the 70s. High\npressure will be in control Monday for fair weather and highs\nranging from the mid 70s north to near 80 degrees across the\nsouth. A cold front will cross Monday night into Tuesday, while\nlatest guidance suggest there will be limited moisture leading\nto mostly fair weather again on Tuesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEarly next week, a surface low over Manitoba will deepen and\nlift northeastward across James Bay into Quebec Wednesday night\nthrough Thursday. The surface low will miss the region to the \nnorth, but the attendant cold front will approach from the\nnorthwest Wednesday. Models suggest deep moisture will advect\npoleward ahead of the front with ensemble means showing PWATs\naround 1.50 inches. Will have to monitor timing of the front as\nmodels spread timing between Wednesday evening and Thursday\nmorning. Sharp height falls with the approaching front suggests\nthere could be sufficient instability for thunderstorms\ndepending on the timing of the front. Machine learning guidance\nout of CSU is picking up on the signal for storms both Wednesday\nand Thursday. Latest NBM brings chance PoPs to much of the area\nwith likely PoPs Wednesday night.",
